#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097539} :activity/id #uuid "f8b1dccc-f3ff-5ada-94f6-8903e447e723" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048408 :topic/id #uuid "9c93fb65-dfac-5ee4-a541-7a3af1e1a39d" :topic/math-academy-id 1150} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097846 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=-f(x)$?\n\n![](images/5b/5bab57ba6ac7cd9b0ea80287457ddc7ee50cadf2580a84a802fa039bbca27e1a.png)\n\nAs usual, It's helpful to first find a few points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$. You can use the points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to identify corresponding points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on }y=f(x) & \\text{ Points on }y=-f(x) \\\\ (-1,2) & (-1,-2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0,1) \\\\ (2,0) & (2,0) \\\\ (3,4) & (3,-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a point on the graph of $y=-f(x)$ with the *same* $x$-value and the *opposite* $y$-value. \n\nLet's plot our new points, and draw $y=-f(x)$.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1e6c5a95dff6b8e1f7eba5de197ba61f0ae64a94db2eeb57fc6cd23661f877.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a **reflection across the $x$-axis** of the graph of $y=f(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "47f1f319-6405-55bb-b469-23e90a480d3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3281 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a23fce47-b188-576e-bce0-7771852090dc" :step/math-academy-id 18021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096477 :question/id #uuid "92bc7054-dfe9-539d-a27d-33207aade624" :question/math-academy-id "e-4976"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef0d732d-f50b-5179-9a77-1378a84173ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097059} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921